---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import RowEditActions from '../../components/RowEditActions.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const rowEditMarkup = `<table class="bo-data-table" data-row-edit>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Qty</th><th scope="col"><span class="bo-visually-hidden">Actions</span></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr data-row-id="WIDGET-A">
      <td>Widget A</td>
      <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">
        <input class="bo-input bo-input--seamless bo-input--numeric" value="12" aria-label="Quantity for Widget A" />
      </td>
      <td class="bo-data-table__row-edit-actions">
        <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--sm bo-btn--icon bo-btn--secondary" type="button" data-row-edit-save aria-label="Save Widget A — unsaved changes" hidden><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--save" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
        <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--sm bo-btn--icon bo-btn--ghost" type="button" data-row-edit-cancel aria-label="Discard changes to Widget A" hidden><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--close" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<script type="module">
  import { initRowEdit } from "@busy-office/ui/js";
  initRowEdit();
  document.addEventListener("bo:row-save", (e) => {
    // e.detail = { row, rowId } — versioned payload, see JS behaviors → Intent events
  });
</script>`;
---
<Gallery title="Inline editing">
  <p class="demo-note">
    Editing values in place, inside a
    <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>data table</a>: seamless
    cell inputs, per-row dirty state, Save/Cancel, and the events your
    app persists with. The component-level how-to — the assembled
    screen, with its data contract and states, is the
    <a href={base + '/patterns/editable-grid'}>editable grid pattern</a>.
    <strong>Not for creating a record</strong> — in-place editing assumes
    the row already exists and that each change is a small correction to
    it. A new record usually has required fields, validation that spans
    several of them, and a decision the user can abandon; that belongs in
    a <a href={base + '/patterns/detail-form'}>detail form</a> or a
    <a href={base + '/patterns/wizard'}>wizard</a>, where Cancel means
    nothing was created. An empty row waiting to be filled in a grid gives
    the user no way to say "actually, never mind".
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Inline edit — seamless inputs in cells</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" style="max-inline-size: 24rem">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Qty</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Widget A</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">
              <input class="bo-input bo-input--seamless bo-input--numeric" value="12" aria-label="Quantity for Widget A" />
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Widget B</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">
              <input class="bo-input bo-input--seamless bo-input--numeric" value="4" aria-label="Quantity for Widget B" />
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The cell is <em>always</em> a real input
    (<code>.bo-input--seamless</code>): it reads as text until hover/focus, so keyboard
    and screen-reader semantics never flip between display and edit modes. Persist with
    a form submit or <code>hx-patch</code> on change.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Multi-row inline edit — dirty state + save/cancel</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">This is the <strong>simple</strong> starting
    point of the editable-table path — plain cells, dirty badge,
    Save/Cancel. The graded continuation (combobox lookup cells, every
    cell type, realtime totals, save-per-change, a full PO line-items
    editor) lives on the
    <a href={base + '/patterns/editable-grid'}>Editable grid pattern</a>.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" style="max-inline-size: 28rem">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-row-edit>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Qty</th><th scope="col"><span class="bo-visually-hidden">Actions</span></th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr data-row-id="WIDGET-A">
            <td>Widget A</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">
              <input class="bo-input bo-input--seamless bo-input--numeric" value="12" aria-label="Quantity for Widget A (editable row)" />
            </td>
            <RowEditActions row="Widget A" />
          </tr>
          <tr data-row-id="WIDGET-B">
            <td>Widget B</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">
              <input class="bo-input bo-input--seamless bo-input--numeric" value="4" aria-label="Quantity for Widget B (editable row)" />
            </td>
            <RowEditActions row="Widget B" />
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Same seamless input as above, plus
    <code>data-row-edit</code> on the table: typing marks the row
    <code>data-row-state="dirty"</code> (amber tint, same visual channel the
    error row state uses) and reveals that row's badge + Save/Cancel. Cancel
    resets the row's inputs to their last-saved value; Save dispatches a
    <code>bo:row-save</code> event with the row and its <code>data-row-id</code>
    — your code persists it (fetch, <code>hx-patch</code>, form submit), this
    behavior only tracks dirty state. Try it: edit a quantity below.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Choosing the editing design</h2>
    <p>Three editing designs ship, split by what the screen's <em>job</em>
    is — pick by context, not by taste:</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">The screen's job</th>
          <th scope="col">Design</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Entry — the user came to fill things in</td>
            <td>Bordered fields: a form (<a href={base + '/patterns/detail-form'}>detail-form</a>) or an editable grid (<a href={base + '/patterns/editable-grid'}>editable-grid</a>)</td>
            <td>The border is the affordance — when entry is the job, every enterable cell should say "you can type here"</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Reading — corrections are occasional</td>
            <td>Seamless cells + row Save/Cancel (this page; <a href={base + '/patterns/detail-form'}>detail-form's field-per-row variant</a>)</td>
            <td>The value reads as a fact until touched; chrome would shout "form" at a screen that is a record</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Entry, but it must LOOK like the read view</td>
            <td>The editable grid's <a href={base + '/patterns/editable-grid'}>WYSIWYG variant</a> — every control seamless</td>
            <td>Display-identical at rest, affordance on hover/focus; the control never swaps, so keyboard and screen-reader semantics never change</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Per field type (date, number, money,
    quantity), the <a href={base + '/concepts/design-language'}>field
    matrix</a> names the exact control for each of these contexts.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Save timing — the second, independent axis</h2>
    <p>Editing design says what a cell <em>looks</em> like; save timing says
    when the edit <em>commits</em>. They are chosen separately, and the second
    one is where screens drift apart. <strong>Not a taste question</strong> —
    the rule is what a row IS.</p>

    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">If a row is…</th>
          <th scope="col">Use</th>
          <th scope="col">Because</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>one record</strong> — a PO line, an item, a timesheet entry</td>
            <td><strong>Batch per row</strong> — <code>data-row-edit</code>, the default. Save/Cancel appear on the dirty row.</td>
            <td>Each row commits alone, so a 422 comes back on the row the user just
            touched and a conflict on one row is not a screen-wide rollback.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>one field of one record</strong> — a settings list, a vendor master</td>
            <td><strong>Form-level save</strong> — one Save for the record. Keep <code>data-row-edit</code> for dirty marking only.</td>
            <td>Six fields means six requests for one logical edit, and a Cancel that
            undoes a third of what the user changed. Measured on an early version of
            <a href={base + '/patterns/detail-form'}>detail-form</a>: a Save button
            <strong>228px from the field it saved</strong>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>independent, with nothing to cancel as a set</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Live</strong> — <code>data-row-edit="live"</code>. No buttons at all.</td>
            <td>Every committed change saves itself. The honest cost: it commits per
            <em>field</em>, so a half-typed line reaches your validation mid-row, and
            you owe per-field success/failure feedback.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>The mistake this table exists to prevent</strong>
    is using per-row save on a list of one record's fields — it looks like a grid and
    is a form. If you cannot name what one row is a record <em>of</em>, you want
    form-level save.</p>

    <h3>How a dirty row says so</h3>
    <p>The framework paints the state; you must not add a fourth statement of it.
    A dirty row carries <strong>two visual channels</strong> — an amber tint and a
    3px inset leading edge — and both are deliberate: under
    <code>forced-colors</code> the tint disappears and the edge becomes a real
    border, so the state survives high-contrast mode.</p>
    <p><strong>Colour is never the whole signal.</strong>
    <code>data-row-state</code> is a data attribute and is invisible to assistive
    technology, so the programmatic channel is the <strong>Save button's accessible
    name</strong> — it exists only while the row is dirty, so its presence and its
    name both carry the meaning. Name it per row
    (<code>Save Bearing kit — unsaved changes</code>), never just "Save": a table of
    identical "Save" buttons gives a screen-reader user nothing to tell them apart,
    and axe cannot catch it because the buttons <em>are</em> named.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">A visible "Unsaved" badge is <strong>not</strong> the
    shape to copy — the row already says it twice, and the buttons appearing at all
    say it a third time. The <code>[data-row-edit-dirty]</code> hook still works if
    your screen genuinely needs a visible word.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup — multi-row inline edit</h2>
    <pre><code>{rowEditMarkup}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <script>
    import { initRowEdit } from '@busy-office/ui/js';
    initRowEdit();
  </script>

  <Related links={[
    ['/patterns/editable-grid', 'Editable grid (the screen)'],
    ['/components/data-table', 'Data table'],
    ['/components/money', 'Money field'],
    ['/concepts/concurrency', 'Concurrency & conflicts'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
